prompt:
Issue #731: redactCredentialData leaves numeric values that are registered credentials

## Problem
`redactCredentialData` (src/util/proc.ts) redacts string values and keys that contain a registered credential, but leaves numbers alone. A credential made only of digits that a JSON number represents exactly is stored and published in full when it appears as a number. This was found in the review of #730 (worker agent-job events) and reproduced on main:

```ts
registerCredential("NUM_SECRET", "987654321012345");
redactCredentialData({ input: { token: 987654321012345, s: "987654321012345" } });
// → {"input":{"token":987654321012345,"s":"[redacted]"}}
```

The helper is shared, so every path that relies on it has the gap: agent events from the local harnesses (`tool_call.input`), stored events, MCP results and worker events.

## Requirements
- In `redactCredentialData`, a number whose canonical string form (`String(n)`) contains a registered credential, or is contained in one (the same rule that applies to strings), becomes the string `"[redacted]"`. Other numbers are unchanged.
- Tests:
  - an all-digit credential as a number, nested in an object and in an array, is redacted;
  - unrelated numbers are unchanged;
  - an all-digit credential that is too large to be an exact JSON number still redacts as a string.

## Out of scope
Other encodings (#335), credential registration rules.

Budget: about 10 lines plus tests.

Closes #731

findings.detail:
Thank you: the diagnosis is right. `echo $$ > gate-pid` creates the file before the shell writes to it, so `wait(() => existsSync(file))` followed by `Number(readFileSync(file))` can read an empty file. `Number("")` is 0, and `recoveryProcessStopped(0)` reports stopped, which matches CI's `Expected: false, Received: true`. Waiting for a complete `^[1-9]\d*\n$` line fixes it.

The same race is in the other tests in this file that read a PID file the same way, and two of them are the other Linux CI flakes named in #851's comments:
- `a canonical-path start recovers an earlier symlink job before unlocking the worktree`: `wait(() => existsSync(pidFile))`, then `Number(readFileSync(pidFile))`, around lines 252–254; it failed on the default branch at 55b69f67;
- `executor crash fallback restores committed bytes after stopping the original check`: the pid is written by `echo $$ > fallback-pid` and read later, around line 316;
- the `gate-pid` wait around line 560;
- the `previous-pid` waits around lines 908–909 and 960.

Add one helper in this file, for example `waitForPid(path)`, which waits (same 10 s bound) until the file holds a complete positive-integer line and returns the number. Use it at every one of these sites, and keep the job-state and setup conditions you added for the detached-cancel case. Don't weaken any assertion, add retries or lengthen timeouts.

Test: a unit check of the helper that writes the file in two steps (create empty, then write the line) and shows `waitForPid` returns only after the line is complete. A plain `existsSync` wait returns early in the same setup. Run only test/gate-inplace-executor.test.ts, `bunx biome check` on the changed file and `bunx tsc --noEmit`. Never run the full suite in a factory run (AGENTS.md).

